REVIEW GUIDE -- how to write the daily automated algo review
============================================================

Read this file, then review_corrections.txt, at the start of every run. The scheduled task
prompt is the driver; this file is the detail and the style contract. Zach edits both this
file and review_corrections.txt as he corrects the output, so THIS FILE WINS over habit.

1. WHAT THE REVIEW IS
---------------------
A tight, skimmable account of how yesterday's EOD algos actually behaved, aimed at three
decisions Zach makes: make dynamic edge sizing more/less aggressive, change the backtest or
prod infra when they disagree, and size a strategy up or down.

It is NOT a log dump, not a tutorial, and not a hedge. If a fact is not verified, leave it
out entirely (see section 6).

TWO HARD RULES, from corrections #3-#8. These outrank everything else in this file:

  SCOPE: every line must be about a script in run_algos.sh. Anything else gets deleted, even
  if it is true and short. No whole-book PnL, no non-algo strategies, no prior-day
  corroboration, no root-cause derivation, no reasoning about what a fix would be.

  FLAG, DO NOT ADJUDICATE: state the finding and stop. Where it matters, use his severity
  words -- "Needs attention." / "May need attention." / "THIS SHOULD BE REVIEWED." Never
  write "cost nothing", "was the good outcome", "correctly", or any verdict that closes the
  question for him. He makes the call; the review surfaces the fact.

The review exists to SAVE HIM TIME. A shorter review that flags the right three things beats
a complete one he has to read twice.

2. INPUTS, IN READING ORDER
---------------------------
a) packs/<YYYY_MM_DD>/digest.txt   -- every number, already computed. Read this FIRST and
   in full. It contains: input freshness, prod-vs-backtest PnL by strategy, PnL streaks,
   market state and trade-gate distances, fills by wave, fill verdicts, V8 size-ratio check,
   sprc-vs-NBBO flags, slowness flags, per-script inventory with order activity and errors.
b) packs/<YYYY_MM_DD>/<script>_clean.txt -- the cleaned prod logs. Read 350pm_script and
   355pm_script every day. Read any other script the digest flagged (error lines, an
   unexplained gap, an unfilled order, a missing log). Skim the rest.
c) On demand only: fill_price_review, prod_change_logs, git history (section 5).
d) The 355PM hacker check lives INSIDE the digest's 355pm_script and 355pm_script_v8 blocks
   as a "355 HACKER -- prod vs hacker backtest" sub-block. It prints ONLY when prod has a
   HACK_ row in algo_trade_log for that script or the hacker backtest
   (Downloads/hacking_355_backtest_cap*_last5_*.xlsx, built 8:19 by CreateBacktestsHacker at
   prod's HACK_MAX_PRICE, --all-parents, --last-days 5) attempted a hack on the review date.
   No sub-block = no hack activity = write nothing about hacking. When it prints, the review
   gets ONE hacker line in that script's block: what prod sent/filled at what debit, what the
   model did, and any line under "HACKER FLAGS". Only the flags are discrepancies (section 7
   lists what is by design).

The digest is derived, not authoritative. When the digest and a log disagree, the log wins
-- and say so in the review, because it means build_review_pack.py needs a fix.

3. STRUCTURE OF THE OUTPUT FILE
-------------------------------
Write D:/PycharmProjects/scratch/algos/automated_algo_reviews/<MM_DD_YYYY>.txt, where the
date is the TRADE DATE REVIEWED (not today). ASCII only, CRLF. Overwrite on a re-run.

HARD WRAP AT 120 CHARACTERS (correction #1). No line in the .txt may exceed 120 chars --
wrap a long bullet onto continuation lines indented 2 spaces so the "- " bullets stay
scannable. This applies ONLY to the .txt. Do NOT wrap the Google Chat message; Chat inserts
its own line breaks, so send that text unwrapped.

Line 1:   PROD PNL <x>k, Backtest PNL <y>k
          Totals across the algo strategies only. If prod or backtest is unavailable, say
          which and why on that line instead of guessing a number.

Then one block per script that traded, most important first (350pm_script and 355pm_script
lead unless something else dominated the day). Each block:

    <script_name>:
    - <finding>
    - <finding>

Findings, in this order when they apply:
  1. Slowness / data-pull problems. If clean, one line: "No notable slow data issues."
  2. Fills: which wave, whether anything was underfilled, and what that implies about edge
     aggressiveness. Name the option and the sprc-vs-fill or sprc-vs-NBBO numbers.
  3. Reconciliation: lead with the AGGREGATE prod vs backtest (correction #14). If the
     aggregate reconciles and no algo breaches the #7 materiality bar, the whole thing is
     "Backtest and prod agree." -- no PnL pair, no lot counts, no idx_at_trade comparison
     (correction #18). Spend detail only on the algos that actually disagree, and decompose
     the gap (CLOSER precedence, loss-cap pro-rata, fills) per correction #15.
  4. Sizing signal: NEVER as a stats readout (correction #11). A streak earns words only if
     it changes a sizing decision, and then it goes inside that strategy's bullet with the
     action -- never as its own "N up days in a row, +$X" line.
  5. Anything unusual: config/infra behavior, caps, month-end/MOC. Two things that are NOT
     findings: a v8 size that is what V8_SIZE_RATIO implies once floored (correction #19), and
     a near-miss outside the tight bars -- under 5 bps, under one vol point, or under $100M
     from a trigger (correction #17). The digest's TRADE-GATE DISTANCES section prints only
     gates inside those bars; if it says write no near-miss line, write none.

Last block, verbatim shape:

    Didn't trade/does not need human review: <script>.py, <script>.py, ...

A script with a genuine finding (error, unexplained slowness, tried-and-unfilled) does NOT
belong on that line even if it made no money -- give it its own short block.

EXCEPTION, 4pm_script_v3 (correction #20): it earns a block ONLY for an execution error or an
open watch item in section 8 naming it. Normal condor fills, resends that eventually fill, and
expiry settlement -- however large the PnL -- go on the didn't-need-review line. This is a
higher bar than the other scripts get.

4. VOICE
--------
Match the two seed reviews in this folder (08_17_2026.txt, 08_18_2026.txt). Concretely:

  - Bullets are short declarative sentences. Numbers inline. No preamble, no wrap-up.
  - State the mechanism, not the category: "0 fills because both target prices were below
    min price so the target rounded up to min price" beats "unfilled due to pricing".
  - Quantify: "Fill price 1.17 vs sprc 1.083 on the 7750 call" beats "filled well".
  - Draw the conclusion Zach would draw, in his terms: "Doesn't point to a need to make
    edge targets more aggressive." / "Needs attention."
  - Counterfactuals are welcome when the day was a near-miss, with the distance: "had the
    market been ~3bps lower we would have been in a DOWN market and IMBAL_VS_MARKET would
    have triggered". Take the distance from the digest's MARKET STATE AND TRADE-GATE
    DISTANCES section, which computes it for every gate -- and only call something a
    near-miss if that section marks the binding gate CLOSE. A veto line in the log
    ("*Don't trade X* because <veto>") is evaluation order, not near-ness: see correction
    #16. The closest gate is often market type or a vol bin edge, not imbal.
  - No em-dashes, no unicode, no markdown headers/bold inside the .txt.
  - Whole review target: under ~40 lines. If it runs longer, cut commentary, not facts.

5. WHEN TO DIG (and when not to)
--------------------------------
Escalate only on a specific unexplained fact, never routinely:

  - fill_price_review: run
      python D:/agent_projects/fill_price_review/fill_price_review.py <ALGO> <YYYY-MM-DD>
    when an order went unfilled or filled oddly and the digest cannot explain why. It needs
    the SpiderRock VPN (198.102.4.55:3307). If the VPN is down, say the second-by-second
    check was not available and move on -- do not speculate in its place.
  - prod_change_logs: read D:/agent_projects/eod_option_algos/prod_change_logs/ before
    suggesting any size change, so the review never proposes reverting a deliberate one.
  - git log on a config in D:/PycharmProjects/scratch/algos/configs*/ when a trigger or
    size looks different from what the backtest assumed.
  - The reconcile-backtests skill: load it when a discrepancy needs real chasing. Its
    known-divergence checklist covers most daily gaps -- min-price-on-replay, SR uPrc vs
    ThetaData index straddling the $1 min-strike-distance gate, CLOSER-takes-precedence
    over BUMPED, loss-cap pro-rata that no backtest models, month-end and Fed exclusions.

Do not run a full backtest, rebuild combined data, or start any multi-minute job other than
the regen path the task prompt authorizes.

6. THE CERTAINTY RULE
---------------------
This review can drive a sizing or config change, so a wrong conclusion is worse than no
conclusion. State as fact only what a log line, DB row, workbook cell, or code path shows.
Everything unverified is OMITTED -- no speculation and no hedge labels either. "Consistent
with" is the strongest phrase allowed for an arithmetic fit that is not directly confirmed.

Where a number in the review came from a specific place, name the place compactly inline
(file, table, or workbook), the way the seed reviews name the option and the price.

7. KNOWN-GOOD PATTERNS (do not report these as problems)
--------------------------------------------------------
  - A 3s pause after wave-1 send: the orchestrator's designed sleep for SR to work orders.
    The concurrent hacker has the same 3s window after ITS wave-2; since 9/10/26 it announces
    itself ("[hacker] HACK_... sleeping 3s for SR to work wave-2", hacker_trades.py) and the
    digest's announced-sleep windows cover it. In logs BEFORE 9/10/26 that pause shows as an
    UNEXPLAINED ~2.9s gap after "Phase 6 done" ending in a fill-check SELECT -- designed wait,
    not a stall. Only a gap OVER ~4s there is real. The
    concurrent hacker task can print inside it ("[hacker] no adversarial hackers survived gate");
    since 9/3/26 the digest tracks announced sleep windows so this no longer shows as UNEXPLAINED.
  - A 60s pause at the end: algo_trade_logger waiting for SR fills to settle.
  - Long idle stretches between 15:32, 15:40, 15:50, 15:55, 16:00: scheduled phase waits.
  - The first query of a phase showing a large conn= time: connection-pool miss, expected.
  - A 1-lot order at a $0.15 credit going unfilled at a $0.30 min_price: the min_price
    floor, working as designed. Worth ONE line, not analysis.
  - btic_and_imbal_script printing hundreds of poll lines ~2-4s apart: its poll cadence.
  - v8 atmVol printing far below v7's at the same snap (e.g. 0.050 vs 0.109 on 9/1, 0.046 vs
    0.099 on 8/28): KNOWN SpiderRock-side bug, fix promised 9/12/26. Do NOT flag it in any
    review through 9/12/26. On the first review AFTER 9/12, check v7-vs-v8 atmVol once and
    report whether the fix landed -- then delete this entry (fixed) or resume flagging (not).
    Note the bug is in the vol PRINT; if a v8 TRADE decision visibly turned on the bad vol
    (a regime-edge or BUMPED gate flip), that trade impact is still reportable.
  - A ~1s pause right after a "Sending start message to chat" / "extra spread orders" line:
    the Google Chat webhook round trip, at its normal cost. Anything materially above that is
    NOT benign -- see WATCH ITEM 1 in section 8.
  - The main backtest showing $5-$35 on a strategy prod never filled: the backtest's
    min-price replay on a 1-lot, not a missed signal.

  - The 340PM/350PM OUTRIGHT family showing no backtest row: create_backtests.py filters
    every OUTRIGHT config unless --outright is passed. Structural and already understood --
    do NOT spend a line on it.

  - EQUITY_ALGO_42 350PM FIRST TICK LIMIT EXIT: never deployed in prod (no PNL LOG NEW.xlsx
    row ever). An equity-backtest row with no prod row for it is structural -- do NOT report
    it as a Cat 1.

  - IMBAL_GROWS CLOSER or BUMPED showing a backtest fire with no prod trade, WITH veto
    evidence in the 350 logs: since 9/9/26 prod vetoes BOTH layers when the projected 3:55pm
    levered-ETF rebal is >4.5B adverse to the sold side (backtests model the gate at 5.0B).
    Veto evidence = the "!!!!... IMBAL_GROWS_CLOSER <Call|Put> VETOED: projected 355 rebal"
    / "IMBAL_GROWS_BUMPED <Call|Put> VETOED" banners or the "[closer_spread] ... CLOSER
    VETOED" line in logs/<date>_350pm_script*.txt. Report it as ONE line naming the gate
    ("GROWS CLOSER backtest-only fire -- prod rebal-vetoed at +X.XB"), not as a Cat 1.
    Two structural sub-cases, also not Cat 1: adverse rebal in (4.5, 5.0] = prod vetoes but
    the backtest trades (threshold gap, expected); and small value drift between prod's live
    ~15:49 estimate and the backtest's 15:50-snap rebal pipeline. On a vetoed-CLOSER day the
    parent trades at FULL size (no closer decrement), so a parent-size-larger-than-backtest
    mismatch on that day is part of the same known pattern. A missing prod trade WITHOUT a
    VETOED line is still reportable as usual. Full detail:
    eod_option_algos/prod_change_logs/9_9_2026.md.

  - 355 HACKER prod-vs-backtest differences that are BY DESIGN (never flag these; the digest
    already names them instead of flagging):
      1. Trigger threshold: backtest |imbal_355| > $1B on T+1 data; prod 950M on the live
         feed. Inside $0.9-1.1B the two can legitimately disagree on whether a hack fires.
      2. Fill price/timing: backtest fills at the 15:55:03 cross + slippage under the cap,
         then a resting bid at exactly the cap to 16:00; prod fires two edge-priced waves
         ~15:55:02-05 plus a wave-3 rest at the cap. Prod fills are usually BETTER.
      3. Size basis: backtest hack_size = floor(model size / 2); prod = floor(actual filled
         size / 2), and the v8 book is ratio-sized (~20%).
      4. Backtest assumes every modeled leg filled at 3:50; prod only hacks legs with
         size_filled > 0, and skips 1-lot fills (half-size 0).
    The FOUR real discrepancies, which the digest prints under "HACKER FLAGS": TRIGGER
    MISMATCH outside the buffer, CAP VIOLATION (avg debit above HACK_MAX_PRICE), SIZE
    RELATION (sent != floor(parent filled / 2)), and POSSIBLE WAVE-3 MALFUNCTION (section 8).

Anything the digest labels "UNEXPLAINED" deserves a look, but look before reporting. Timed
waits are read straight out of the prod script (the digest names which script and how many
marks it found), so an UNEXPLAINED gap is usually a pause that is not clock-driven at all: a
webhook round trip, an async send, a slow query. Say which it is. If it does turn out to be a
timed wait the parser missed, say so -- that means wait_marks_for_script() needs fixing, not
the fallback list.

8. OPEN WATCH ITEMS
-------------------
Things we have already seen once and want caught if they recur. These are NOT in the
known-good list -- flag them, with the prior observation for comparison, so a one-off can be
told apart from a trend. Zach adds and retires items here.

WATCH ITEM 1 -- Google Chat recap post latency (first seen 2026-08-20)
  What to measure: the gap between the recap post and the line after it. In the 350 scripts
  that is "Finished extra spread orders" -> "Starting algo_trade_logger (60s wait for SR
  fills)". The only network call in between is send_message() to Google Chat; the summary
  build itself is string formatting, and myGetSpyEquivalent only runs when a trade fired.

  Baseline and what we saw:
      date    350pm_script (v7)    350pm_script_v8
      8/18          1.11s               1.34s
      8/19          1.05s               2.23s
      8/20          1.02s               9.99s   <- the observation
  v7 sits on ~1.0s every day. The 8/20 v8 outlier is 10x that.

  FLAG IT IF: any script's recap post exceeds ~3s, or v7's ~1.0s baseline moves. Report the
  measured span and compare it to the table above -- one slow day is noise, a second one is a
  trend and Zach wants to know.

  What NOT to claim: on 8/20 this cost nothing. No orders were live, the stall sits after the
  send phase, and algo_trade_logger sleeps 60s next anyway, so the 10s consumed slack that was
  already idle. Do not report it as a trading impact on a day where nothing was pending.

  The reason it is on this list anyway: send_message (D:/PycharmProjects/utils/
  google_chat_webhook.py:29) builds a fresh httplib2.Http() per call with NO timeout, never
  reads the response, and has no retry. So there is no bound on how long a hung POST can block,
  and on a day with orders working that stall would sit between the send phase and the
  fill-check. Best guess for the 8/20 event was a dropped TCP SYN (Windows ~3s initial RTO
  doubling to ~6s, which lands near the observed 9.99s), but nothing times the POST itself so
  that was never confirmed.

  Decision as of 2026-08-21: Zach does NOT want the code changed for now. google_chat_webhook
  is a shared library in D:/PycharmProjects/utils with 69 referencing files, so a fix needs a
  signature-compatible change and a heads-up to the team. Do not propose the fix again in a
  daily review -- just flag the recurrence and point at this item.

